+
+## JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch, PatchError } from "json-toolkit";
+
+applyPatch({ foo: "bar" }, [{ op: "add", path: "/baz", value: "qux" }]); // { foo: "bar", baz: "qux" }
+```
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies the operations `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test` in order
+and returns the result as a new value that shares no structure with the inputs; neither `document` nor `patch` is
+mutated. It is atomic: on error nothing is returned. It throws a `PatchError` whose `code` is `"INVALID_PATCH"`
+(patch not an array, unknown `op`, missing or ill-typed `path`/`from`/`value`, invalid pointer) or
+`"OPERATION_FAILED"` (missing target, bad array index, `move` into its own child, failed `test`). `test` compares
+JSON values deeply (object member order is irrelevant). `__proto__`, `constructor` and `prototype` are ordinary
+member names and cannot pollute prototypes.
